<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  The build cache's configuration (org.apache.maven.extensions:maven-build-cache-extension, loaded by
  `.mvn/extensions.xml`). Deliberately minimal: every knob left at its default is a behaviour nobody had to
  reason about, and the defaults here are the ones the extension's own schema documents.

  WHAT IS NOT CONFIGURED, AND WHY. In particular the `input/global/glob` that decides which files are hashed is
  left alone. Widening it looks safer — this repository's modules also decide their output from `.js` and `.json`
  inputs — but a glob the extension's matcher does not read the way I assume would match *nothing*, and a cache
  that hashes nothing answers every build with the first revision it ever saw. That is the one failure this file
  must not cause, so the default is kept and the behaviour is verified instead: turning a source file into a
  change must produce a cache miss for its module and its dependents, and that is what the round's evidence
  shows (see the plan's infrastructure record).

  THE GATE IS NOT CACHED. A cache hit answering with an earlier revision's outputs is the same hazard `clean`
  exists to prevent (F-47). `scripts/lib/gate.js` therefore passes `-Dmaven.build.cache.enabled=false` on every
  invocation and `GateContractTest` asserts it, so "the recorded gate" and "the cache" cannot be confused for one
  another by accident.
-->
<cache xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/BUILD-CACHE-CONFIG/1.0.0"
       xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
       xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/BUILD-CACHE-CONFIG/1.0.0 https://maven.apache.org/xsd/build-cache-config-1.0.0.xsd">
    <configuration>
        <enabled>true</enabled>
        <!-- XX is the default: fast, and a collision here is a wrong build, so the extension's own choice of a
             non-cryptographic hash is kept rather than second-guessed. -->
        <hashAlgorithm>XX</hashAlgorithm>
    </configuration>
</cache>
